notes.body:
A reverse proxy sits close to your servers and can route, balance and cache.

- **Routing by path**: `/api` to one machine, `/static` to another, or to the same machine on different ports.
- **Routing by host**: the IP/TCP connection carries only IP addresses and ports; after `getaddrinfo()` you only have an IP. The DNS name you used must therefore be sent again inside HTTP as the **`Host` header**, which is why the earlier Google telnet demo needed `Host: google.com`. One IP can then serve ten different domains or subdomains, and the proxy routes on the Host value.

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name shop.example.com;          # routing by host
    location /api/   { proxy_pass http://api_pool; }       # routing by path
    location /static/ { root /var/www; }                    # served from disk (sendfile)
}
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name blog.example.com;
    location / { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8081; }
}
```

(Directive syntax shown is standard nginx; the lecture's demo files covered routing, weights, fallbacks and cache.)

short.modelAnswer:
upstream api_pool {
    server 10.0.0.11 weight=3;
    server 10.0.0.12 weight=1;
    server 10.0.0.99 backup;   # static fallback, only used when the others are down
}
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;
    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://api_pool;
    }
    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.php?$args;
    }
}
Weighted round robin sends about 3 of every 4 API requests to the bigger machine; the backup server takes traffic only if both primaries fail; try_files serves existing files and falls back to index.php.
